1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,120
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,740 --> 00:00:11,960
I just spent a few moments looking at a trip report,

3
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:17,840
which is basically like a crew log from our canoe trip in 2004.

4
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:22,160
We had a day of pretty good rain,

5
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:25,260
and we decided that we were going to head out a little bit early

6
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:27,080
because we were pretty wet,

7
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:29,640
and it didn't look like the weather was going to get much better.

8
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:32,600
And so we headed down a huge lake,

9
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:38,040
8 or 10 miles of paddling down this big lake with the wind at our backs,

10
00:00:38,340 --> 00:00:39,700
and it all went pretty well.

11
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:41,040
The waves were a little high,

12
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:45,540
and we were very anxious to have things over with.

13
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:48,640
And we got in sight of the dock there,

14
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:52,080
probably just about 75 yards from the dock,

15
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:55,040
and I was in the stern of our canoe,

16
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:57,360
and one of my assistant Scoutmasters,

17
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:01,260
he was in the front seat of the canoe up in the bow.

18
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:05,720
And we had turned the corner, and we saw the dock,

19
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:09,480
and we're ending this big trip for six or seven days out in the wilderness,

20
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:16,060
and we were really happy to see the Outfitters store there and everything,

21
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:18,880
and we were paddling along quite nicely,

22
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:23,660
and then my partner's canoe paddle broke,

23
00:01:24,460 --> 00:01:25,880
like right in the middle.

24
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:27,780
It just broke.

25
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:29,360
It broke in half.

26
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:30,280
He couldn't do anything.

27
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:32,160
And so I paddled us in.

28
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:35,100
Seven days with that paddle.

29
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:37,240
It broke in the last 75 yards,

30
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:40,180
and we didn't have a spare along with us.

31
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:42,100
You know what we've done since?

32
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:43,300
We carried a spare paddle.

33
00:01:44,140 --> 00:01:48,480
Hey, that's one useful way that these little patrol logs and trip logs work.

34
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:50,920
So we'll be talking about it a little bit more.

35
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:53,460
This is podcast number 25.

36
00:01:53,460 --> 00:02:23,440
This is podcast number 25.

37
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:30,060
Welcome back to the Scoutmasters podcast.

38
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:33,620
This is Clark Green, and this is podcast number 25,

39
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:34,940
a day of celebration.

40
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:36,680
Okay, celebration's over.

41
00:02:36,760 --> 00:02:37,660
Let's get down to business.

42
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:40,540
In Scoutmastership, in seven minutes or less,

43
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:43,980
we're going to be talking about patrol logs and their usefulness.

44
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:48,280
And then I have a Scoutmasters minute for Scoutmasters.

45
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:52,060
Not really for Scouts, but for Scoutmasters.

46
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:54,400
And it's not a minute.

47
00:02:55,100 --> 00:02:56,160
It's a lot of minutes.

48
00:02:56,880 --> 00:02:58,260
But you're used to that, aren't you?

49
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:03,020
And then we'll end up with our third installment

50
00:03:03,020 --> 00:03:08,220
in our discussion of building a troop-based high adventure program.

51
00:03:08,220 --> 00:03:10,420
All righty, then.

52
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:12,900
I think we should get with it, don't you?

53
00:03:14,020 --> 00:03:18,480
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

54
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:29,380
Somewhere along the way recently, I mentioned patrol logs,

55
00:03:29,459 --> 00:03:32,200
and I've gotten a couple of questions about the importance of them

56
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:34,280
and how they work and et cetera.

57
00:03:34,459 --> 00:03:36,760
So I want to spend a couple of minutes just talking about

58
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:41,019
how a patrol log is a useful tool to a patrol leader

59
00:03:41,019 --> 00:03:42,280
and a senior patrol leader.

60
00:03:42,799 --> 00:03:44,100
What is a patrol log?

61
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:47,280
Well, basically, it's just a diary of what the patrol does,

62
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:52,420
and it contains some facts about who was participating

63
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:54,700
and what they did and things like that.

64
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:59,200
But most importantly, it should have a place for each member of the patrol

65
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:03,980
to put in their personal reflections on exactly what worked for them

66
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:04,820
and what they enjoyed

67
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:07,780
and perhaps what they didn't enjoy and what didn't work for them.

68
00:04:07,780 --> 00:04:10,160
This way, the patrol leader can review it

69
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:12,060
and the senior patrol leader can review it

70
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:16,160
and they can look at improving the way that they present their program.

71
00:04:17,680 --> 00:04:22,480
Practically, a patrol log is, you know, one of those little composition books

72
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:25,840
and the patrol leader has that with them

73
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:27,800
whenever his patrol meets

74
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:33,620
and the patrol scribe is the one who is charged with keeping up with the log

75
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:35,660
and making sure that things get written in it.

76
00:04:35,660 --> 00:04:39,580
One of the ways that we emphasize the usefulness of the patrol log

77
00:04:39,580 --> 00:04:42,340
is anytime we're camping, whether it's a weekend camp out,

78
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:45,900
whether it's a high adventure trip, or whether it's our week at summer camp,

79
00:04:46,340 --> 00:04:51,000
the patrol log gets opened every evening just for a few minutes.

80
00:04:51,420 --> 00:04:55,260
The patrol sits around and they make a record of what's in their log.

81
00:04:55,820 --> 00:04:59,160
And then when we're on a weekend camping trip, it'll be on a Saturday night.

82
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:00,960
We usually arrive on Fridays.

83
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:04,620
And then on a Saturday night, after dinner is cleaned up,

84
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:09,219
the patrol will sit down and make a record in their log.

85
00:05:09,479 --> 00:05:13,440
And then they will all get together, the entire troop will get together

86
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:15,460
for what we call Vespers,

87
00:05:15,599 --> 00:05:20,179
which is just a little evening observance of a scout's reverence and duty to God.

88
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:24,340
And we use the patrol logs as a way of starting the conversation

89
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:27,539
about what went on today and what worked well and what didn't work.

90
00:05:27,540 --> 00:05:30,680
And this lasts for, you know, 15 or 20 minutes,

91
00:05:30,820 --> 00:05:35,520
but it gives us a way to sit down and to concentrate on what we're doing

92
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:37,060
and why we're doing it.

93
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:43,840
At summer camp, the patrol leader's council will assemble every evening for a few moments

94
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:47,360
after the patrols have made the entries in their logs.

95
00:05:47,660 --> 00:05:50,460
And the patrol leaders will each share what's in the log.

96
00:05:50,460 --> 00:05:56,820
And the patrol, the senior patrol leader will work with them to help correct things that are wrong

97
00:05:56,820 --> 00:05:59,799
or maybe encourage them to do something that they've missed

98
00:05:59,799 --> 00:06:03,240
or congratulate them on a job well done.

99
00:06:03,659 --> 00:06:07,659
And it's also a way for me to sit back kind of quietly and listen

100
00:06:07,659 --> 00:06:12,679
and get a good idea of exactly what's happening during the day at summer camp.

101
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:17,300
And maybe it will bring some things to mind that we need to deal with

102
00:06:17,300 --> 00:06:22,900
and maybe it will give me an opportunity to give the patrols and the patrol leaders

103
00:06:22,900 --> 00:06:25,120
a good pat on the back for a job well done.

104
00:06:25,460 --> 00:06:27,020
It's a pretty simple system.

105
00:06:27,540 --> 00:06:30,960
And it's not something that we require of the scouts.

106
00:06:31,400 --> 00:06:34,480
It's not something that we force upon them and we double check

107
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:37,640
and we read all the patrol logs and we make a big deal out of it.

108
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:41,880
But it's more or less a tool that they can use and we encourage them to use it.

109
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:44,740
And we don't get all upset and angry when they don't.

110
00:06:46,219 --> 00:06:49,560
But we encourage them to use it and to make a good habit out of it.

111
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:51,460
Does it work all the time?

112
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:52,740
No, absolutely not.

113
00:06:52,820 --> 00:06:53,919
It doesn't work all the time.

114
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:56,240
But it does help.

115
00:06:56,880 --> 00:07:02,820
Our high adventure logs probably have been the most useful to us over time

116
00:07:02,820 --> 00:07:06,120
because they helped us capture a lot of little details

117
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:09,100
that have helped us improve those trips over time.

118
00:07:09,660 --> 00:07:12,480
And the scouts really like going back and reading them

119
00:07:12,480 --> 00:07:15,600
because we make a tradition out of, you know,

120
00:07:15,660 --> 00:07:17,400
what was your favorite thing today

121
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:20,400
or what was the most interesting thing that you saw

122
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:23,060
or what was the most difficult part of your day.

123
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:29,360
And it really helps you remember after the trip is over exactly what happened.

124
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:34,600
And it has gone a long way to helping us improve the program

125
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:36,520
around our high adventure trips.

126
00:07:36,620 --> 00:07:38,800
So I encourage you to give it some thought.

127
00:07:39,340 --> 00:07:43,020
Get the patrol leaders and the senior patrol leader to buy into the idea

128
00:07:43,020 --> 00:07:48,160
and see if a patrol log will help you in building your troop program

129
00:07:48,160 --> 00:07:50,120
and building your patrols.

130
00:07:50,120 --> 00:08:18,440
That's right.

131
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:21,620
It's time for a Scoutmaster's Minute.

132
00:08:25,980 --> 00:08:32,679
If I say these two words to you, what do you think?

133
00:08:32,780 --> 00:08:34,000
I've got some Borglund.

134
00:08:35,080 --> 00:08:36,720
No, it's not Cub Scout code.

135
00:08:36,940 --> 00:08:39,000
No, I'm not cursing at you in a foreign language.

136
00:08:39,640 --> 00:08:40,140
Listen again.

137
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:42,020
Got some Borglund.

138
00:08:44,300 --> 00:08:44,600
Yeah?

139
00:08:44,940 --> 00:08:45,740
Doesn't ring a bell?

140
00:08:45,740 --> 00:08:50,320
Well, maybe somewhere back in the deep recesses of your memory

141
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:52,320
that name is stored back there somewhere.

142
00:08:52,780 --> 00:08:53,720
Heck of a name, isn't it?

143
00:08:53,780 --> 00:08:54,060
Got some?

144
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:56,280
Yeah.

145
00:08:56,900 --> 00:08:58,540
G-U-T-Z-O-N.

146
00:08:58,740 --> 00:08:59,880
Got some Borglund.

147
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:04,100
Well, you might not remember the name,

148
00:09:04,660 --> 00:09:05,920
but you know this guy.

149
00:09:06,360 --> 00:09:07,140
You certainly do.

150
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:11,660
Now, Got some probably not a good person to use as an example of a hero

151
00:09:11,660 --> 00:09:13,120
in some ways.

152
00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:15,300
He had some troublesome politics in his time.

153
00:09:16,140 --> 00:09:17,460
He was irascible.

154
00:09:17,700 --> 00:09:18,860
He was a perfectionist.

155
00:09:19,100 --> 00:09:20,820
He was difficult to get along with.

156
00:09:21,140 --> 00:09:24,260
But you know some of his work.

157
00:09:25,060 --> 00:09:27,180
You absolutely know some of his work.

158
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:29,900
Who is he?

159
00:09:30,640 --> 00:09:31,180
Well, look.

160
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:32,959
This is why he came to mind.

161
00:09:32,959 --> 00:09:36,140
The other night, we were at a scout meeting,

162
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:38,959
and we were having our little patrol leader's council afterwards,

163
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:43,040
and I was working away with the guys.

164
00:09:43,199 --> 00:09:46,120
I was encouraging them and trying to coach them

165
00:09:46,120 --> 00:09:48,160
and work with them a little bit.

166
00:09:49,939 --> 00:09:51,979
And it was, you know,

167
00:09:52,079 --> 00:09:54,660
it was like emptying a bathtub with a teaspoon.

168
00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:58,740
You know exactly what I mean, right?

169
00:09:58,860 --> 00:09:59,699
You think,

170
00:09:59,819 --> 00:10:01,819
well, are we going to have to talk about this again?

171
00:10:02,339 --> 00:10:04,800
Do we have to go over this ground one more time?

172
00:10:05,760 --> 00:10:06,660
Well, you do.

173
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:09,420
And that's what Got some did.

174
00:10:09,780 --> 00:10:09,980
Yeah.

175
00:10:10,180 --> 00:10:12,180
You got to keep chipping away at things.

176
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:15,900
It's one little chip here,

177
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:17,199
one little chip there,

178
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:20,080
and finally you have something in the end

179
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:22,560
that you've chipped away out of the solid rock.

180
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,200
And this is what came to mind after,

181
00:10:25,400 --> 00:10:25,700
you know,

182
00:10:25,740 --> 00:10:28,240
we sat through five or ten minutes of listening,

183
00:10:28,540 --> 00:10:31,560
and then I talked to the guys for a little bit

184
00:10:31,560 --> 00:10:32,800
and asked them lots of questions

185
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:34,000
about what they were doing

186
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:35,060
and why they were doing it,

187
00:10:35,100 --> 00:10:37,780
trying to get to the point of a resolution

188
00:10:37,780 --> 00:10:39,240
and moving ahead.

189
00:10:39,820 --> 00:10:44,440
And as, you know,

190
00:10:44,440 --> 00:10:45,260
as we left,

191
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:47,960
one of my assistant scoutmasters

192
00:10:47,960 --> 00:10:48,820
kind of looked at me

193
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:50,480
and he kind of sighed

194
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:51,080
and shook his head

195
00:10:51,080 --> 00:10:51,420
and I said,

196
00:10:51,480 --> 00:10:52,020
yeah, I know,

197
00:10:52,200 --> 00:10:52,640
I know,

198
00:10:52,740 --> 00:10:54,160
this is like trying to,

199
00:10:54,380 --> 00:10:57,520
this is like trying to build Mount Rushmore.

200
00:11:00,260 --> 00:11:02,040
You just keep chipping away.

201
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:04,540
You get the air hammers out every morning

202
00:11:04,540 --> 00:11:05,880
and you get on a rope

203
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:07,600
and you put yourself down

204
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:08,860
and you start chipping away.

205
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:10,740
And, you know,

206
00:11:10,800 --> 00:11:12,220
maybe every once in a while

207
00:11:12,220 --> 00:11:13,520
you can get out the dynamite,

208
00:11:13,520 --> 00:11:15,079
but probably not

209
00:11:15,079 --> 00:11:16,840
because that'll blow the whole thing to pieces.

210
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:20,020
So it's just constant effort,

211
00:11:20,840 --> 00:11:21,360
consistent.

212
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:24,100
Mount Rushmore took about,

213
00:11:24,340 --> 00:11:24,819
I think,

214
00:11:25,240 --> 00:11:25,600
you know,

215
00:11:25,640 --> 00:11:27,079
closing in on 25 years.

216
00:11:28,380 --> 00:11:30,720
That's who Gutzum Borglund is,

217
00:11:30,900 --> 00:11:31,120
right?

218
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:32,460
He's the sculptor

219
00:11:32,460 --> 00:11:34,880
who created Mount Rushmore.

220
00:11:35,280 --> 00:11:36,620
But he kept chipping away,

221
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:37,040
didn't he?

222
00:11:37,920 --> 00:11:39,579
And that's what we've got today

223
00:11:39,579 --> 00:11:41,439
up in the Black Hills

224
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:44,100
that every single person

225
00:11:44,100 --> 00:11:45,760
who's hearing me

226
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:47,860
has seen at least a picture of.

227
00:11:48,580 --> 00:11:50,040
Our work sometimes

228
00:11:50,040 --> 00:11:50,940
in scouting

229
00:11:50,940 --> 00:11:52,960
is kind of like

230
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:54,520
building Mount Rushmore.

231
00:11:55,480 --> 00:11:55,780
Yeah,

232
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:57,520
just a chip at a time.

233
00:11:58,420 --> 00:11:59,960
You get rid of all the stuff

234
00:11:59,960 --> 00:12:00,860
that you don't need.

235
00:12:00,980 --> 00:12:02,840
You refine the stuff that you do.

236
00:12:03,840 --> 00:12:05,500
And the final result

237
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:06,580
is really

238
00:12:06,580 --> 00:12:08,540
kind of a work of art

239
00:12:08,540 --> 00:12:09,760
and a wonder to behold.

240
00:12:10,160 --> 00:12:11,340
I can imagine somewhere,

241
00:12:11,520 --> 00:12:12,440
like in the 12th year,

242
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:13,640
Gutzum Borglund getting up

243
00:12:13,640 --> 00:12:14,240
in one morning

244
00:12:14,240 --> 00:12:14,800
and saying,

245
00:12:14,900 --> 00:12:15,660
what in the world

246
00:12:15,660 --> 00:12:16,940
have I gotten myself into?

247
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:18,500
But you know,

248
00:12:18,540 --> 00:12:19,220
he kept a vision.

249
00:12:19,860 --> 00:12:20,760
He knew in his mind

250
00:12:20,760 --> 00:12:21,680
what he wanted to create.

251
00:12:22,020 --> 00:12:22,900
He knew how to do it.

252
00:12:23,300 --> 00:12:24,060
He kept at it.

253
00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:25,460
We're creating something

254
00:12:25,460 --> 00:12:26,040
as scout leaders.

255
00:12:26,180 --> 00:12:27,160
We're creating an environment

256
00:12:27,160 --> 00:12:28,480
for boys to grow

257
00:12:28,480 --> 00:12:30,480
and to learn some things

258
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:31,180
about the world

259
00:12:31,180 --> 00:12:32,360
to develop character

260
00:12:32,360 --> 00:12:33,960
and to become

261
00:12:33,960 --> 00:12:35,140
decent human beings.

262
00:12:35,960 --> 00:12:36,580
And it works.

263
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:38,640
Sometimes it takes longer

264
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:39,920
than we think it ought to.

265
00:12:40,580 --> 00:12:41,620
And it's a little bit

266
00:12:41,620 --> 00:12:42,180
more difficult

267
